course: biology
lesson: 257
title: The microscopic world: bacteria, good and bad
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Bacteria are living things made of just one tiny cell, so small that hundreds could sit side by side across the full stop at the end of this sentence; they live everywhere, from deep rocks and clouds to Antarctic ice and your own tummy.
explain: Cartoons draw germs as angry green monsters, but most bacteria are harmless or even helpful, and only a small number make people ill.
explain: The yoghurt in your fridge was made by bacteria that turned milk sugar into acid, and the bacteria in your gut help digest your dinner and make vitamins for you.
explain: When the bad ones do get in, as in a bacterial throat infection, a doctor may give antibiotics, medicines first found when Alexander Fleming noticed in 1928 that a mould on a dish had killed the bacteria around it.
explain: Bacteria multiply by splitting in two, as often as every 20 minutes, which is why milk left out on a warm day goes sour so fast and why food lasts longer in the cold fridge.
fact: Yoghurt is made by bacteria that turn milk sugar into acid, which thickens the milk and makes it sour; bacteria also help make cheese, pickles, sauerkraut and sourdough bread.
fact: Some bacteria cause illness, such as strep throat, and antibiotics can kill them, but antibiotics do nothing against viruses, so they do not help a cold.
fact: Bacteria can become resistant to antibiotics if the medicines are overused, which is why doctors do not give them for colds.
fact: In 1928 Alexander Fleming noticed that a mould on a dish had killed the bacteria around it, which led to penicillin, a medicine that has saved many millions of lives.
fact: About as many bacteria live in and on your body as there are cells of your own, mostly in the gut, and together they weigh about 200 grams.
fact: Gut bacteria help digest food, make some vitamins and protect against harmful germs.
fact: Soap breaks up the greasy layer on germs and lifts them off the skin; washing for about 20 seconds, between fingers and under nails, removes most of them.
fact: In 1847 the doctor Ignaz Semmelweis found that handwashing by doctors saved mothers' lives, but many other doctors laughed at him, because nobody yet knew that germs spread illness.
fact: In good conditions a bacterium can split in two every 20 minutes, so one can become millions in a few hours; a fridge slows bacteria down but does not kill them.
fact: The rainbow colours of hot springs in Yellowstone come from different bacteria living at different temperatures, some in water near boiling.
fact: The bobtail squid carries glowing bacteria on its belly that match the moonlight and hide its shadow from hunters below.
fact: Bacteria and fungi are decomposers: in compost and in soil they break down peelings, dead leaves and dead animals, give off heat as they work, and return nutrients to the soil; a big heap can reach about 60 degrees in the middle.
fact: Billions of years ago, cyanobacteria made the first oxygen in the air by photosynthesis, and the rocks they built, called stromatolites, still grow in Shark Bay, Australia.
fact: Some scientists think meeting many harmless germs outdoors and with animals trains a child's immune system, but this is still studied and never means skipping handwashing before eating or after the toilet.
exercise: What do bacteria do to milk to make yoghurt? | Turn milk sugar into acid, which thickens it | Add tiny bits of fresh fruit to it so that it goes lumpy and thick | Freeze it into a soft paste | Suck all the water out of it
exercise: Which of these foods is made with the help of bacteria? | Sauerkraut | Plain boiled rice | Fresh orange juice | A raw carrot
exercise: What can antibiotics kill? | Bacteria | Viruses | Every germ there is | Only fungi
exercise: Why should you not take antibiotics for a cold? | Colds are caused by viruses, which antibiotics cannot kill | Antibiotics make colds last for years | Colds are caused by eating far too much sugar and too many sweets | A cold is not a real illness
exercise: What can happen if antibiotics are used too much? | Bacteria can become resistant to them | Bacteria become friendly and helpful | People become resistant to bacteria | Antibiotics turn into viruses
exercise: What did Alexander Fleming notice in 1928? | A mould on a dish had killed the bacteria around it | Bacteria could glow in the dark like tiny little lamps | Milk turned to yoghurt by itself | Soap killed all germs instantly
exercise: Where in your body do most of your bacteria live? | In the gut | In the brain | In the bones | In the heart
exercise: Roughly how much do all your gut bacteria weigh together? | About 200 grams, like a small apple | About 2 grams, like a grape | About 2 kilograms, like a bag of sugar | They weigh nothing at all
exercise: How does soap help remove germs? | It breaks up their greasy layer and lifts them off the skin | It freezes the germs to death on the skin | It paints over the germs so that they cannot move about or spread any more | It feeds them so they stop biting
exercise: What did the doctor Ignaz Semmelweis find in 1847? | Handwashing by doctors saved mothers' lives | Mould on a dish could kill the bacteria near it | Vaccines could stop smallpox | Bacteria lived in hot springs
exercise: How often can a bacterium split in two in good conditions? | About every 20 minutes | About once a year | About once a week | About every 20 seconds
exercise: What does a fridge do to bacteria? | Slows them down but does not kill them | Kills every single one of them overnight | Makes them grow faster | Turns them into mould
exercise: What makes the rainbow colours of the hot springs in Yellowstone? | Different bacteria living at different temperatures | Bright paint that is added every year for the tourists | Light bouncing off gold in the rocks | Fish of many colours
exercise: How does the bobtail squid use glowing bacteria? | To match the moonlight and hide its shadow | To light up its way so it can find food in the dark | To scare away other squids | To keep its eggs warm
exercise: Why does a compost heap get warm inside? | Bacteria and fungi give off heat as they break things down | The sun heats the heap from above, just like the inside of an oven | Worms rub together and make heat | Rotting leaves catch fire slowly
exercise: Which living things made the first oxygen in Earth's air? | Cyanobacteria | Dinosaurs | Oak trees | Fish
exercise: Where can you still see stromatolites growing today? | Shark Bay, Australia | The middle of the Sahara Desert | The top of Mount Everest | The bottom of every pond
test: In a cartoon, a germ with angry eyes and sharp teeth attacks a boy. What is true of real bacteria? | They have no faces, eyes or brains, and most are harmless or helpful | They all have little faces, but they are far too small for us to see them | They are all dangerous monsters | They only attack children who are naughty
test: «got a stinking cold, doc better give me antibiotics» What is right? | Antibiotics do not work on cold viruses | Antibiotics cure a cold in an hour | Only strong antibiotics work on colds | Antibiotics make you catch more colds
test: Count them: 1 bacterium splits in two every 20 minutes. How many are there after 1 hour? | 8 | 3 | 6 | 60
test: «my nan says dirt's good for kids, so no need to wash hands before tea» What is right? | Playing outdoors may help, but you should still wash hands before eating | Right, washing your hands is bad for you, so you should never do it at all | Right, dirt is a healthy food | No, children should never go outdoors
test: What would happen if there were no decomposers like bacteria? | Dead leaves and bodies would pile up and soil would lose its nutrients | Nothing would ever die | Plants would grow much faster, with no bacteria about to steal their food | The air would fill with oxygen
test: Why did many doctors in 1847 laugh at Semmelweis? | Nobody yet knew about germs, so handwashing seemed pointless | His own hands were too dirty for anybody to listen | He wanted them to wash their hands in warm milk instead of in water | He was only a child
test: A jug of milk stays on the table all day in summer. Why does it go sour? | Bacteria multiply in the warm milk and make acid | The sunlight coming in turns the milk into lemon juice | Milk always goes sour after noon | Air pushes the cream to the bottom
test: «are we even human then if half of us is bacteria» What is the best answer? | We are human, with a crowd of mostly helpful bacteria living with us | No, we are really bacteria dressed up in a human costume | Yes, but the bacteria in our guts control our brains and choose what we do | No, bacteria cannot live inside people
test: Where would you look for some of the toughest bacteria on Earth? | In near-boiling hot springs and in Antarctic ice | Only in clean hospitals | Only in warm bathrooms | Nowhere, bacteria cannot live in hard places
test: Do we owe the air we breathe partly to bacteria? | Yes, cyanobacteria made the first oxygen billions of years ago | No, oxygen was always in the air, right from the very start of the Earth | No, volcanoes made all of it | Yes, but only the bacteria in our gut
test: Why is a squid's glowing belly like camouflage? | It hides the squid's dark shadow against the moonlit water above | It makes the squid look just like a fish | It blinds the hunter for ever with one very bright flash of white light | It shows the squid's friends where it is
exam: Identify the principal reason for which antibiotics are not prescribed for the common cold. | The common cold is caused by viruses, against which antibiotics are without effect | Antibiotics are effective only during the summer months | The common cold is caused by an excess of bacteria in the gut, which antibiotics remove | Antibiotics render the patient permanently susceptible to colds
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the discovery made by Alexander Fleming in 1928. | A mould upon a culture dish was observed to have destroyed the surrounding bacteria | Bacteria were observed for the first time through a microscope | Handwashing was demonstrated to prevent illness | The first vaccine was prepared from cowpox
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises bacteria in general? | The majority are harmless or beneficial, a small minority being the cause of disease | All bacteria are invariably the cause of disease | Bacteria inhabit only unclean environments, such as drains and refuse | Bacteria are multicellular animals possessing eyes, mouths and minute limbs
exam: Ascertain the effect of refrigeration upon bacteria present in food. | Their multiplication is retarded, although they are not destroyed | They are entirely destroyed within the space of one night | Their multiplication is greatly accelerated | They are transformed into fungi
